course: dictionary
lesson: 1650
title: Chin: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A chin is the part of your face below your mouth, at the front of your lower jaw. Some words for body parts are simple, but chin also shows up in phrases, so the context matters.
explain: Read «she was up to her chin in salt water». Her chin is not a meaning of the water. It shows how deep the water was: it reached all the way up to her face. Ask yourself: does the sentence mean a part of the face, or is it a phrase?
explain: Try swapping the meaning in. «Keep your chin up!» does not mean keep the lower part of your face up. It means stay cheerful and brave. When the swapped meaning makes no sense, you have probably met a phrase.
fact: Chin means the part of the face below the mouth, at the front of the lower jaw.
fact: «Keep your chin up!» means stay cheerful and brave.
fact: «Up to her chin in water» means the water reached her chin, so it was deep.
fact: A chin strap is a strap that buckles under the chin to hold a helmet on.
fact: Electric eels can press their chin against a threat; the scientist Kenneth Catania tested this and wrote about it in 2016 and 2017.
exercise: In «Keep your chin up!», what does chin mean? | A small wooden stick | A soft cushion for the head | *Stay cheerful and brave | A part of the face below the mouth
exercise: «A helmet should sit level, with room for about one finger under the buckled chin strap.» Find and copy one word that names a part of the body. | finger | helmet | strap | *chin
exercise: Which best describes a chin? | *The part of the face below the mouth | The part of the head above the eyes | A tiny bone inside the ear | The part of the leg below the knee
test: In «she was up to her chin in salt water», what does chin show? | Her shoulder | A tiny fish | *The point on her face where the water reached | Her smile
test: Which phrase means stay cheerful and brave? | Chin strap | *Keep your chin up | Chin-up | Double chin
exam: Which sentence uses chin as a part of the face? | «The helmet has a chin strap.» | «Keep your chin up.» | «Splash, she was up to her chin.» | *«She rubs her chin while she thinks.»
